<speak>
<p>Each comparison product has two properties: odd order, and residue one modulo one plus i. We will show that together they force exact equality to one. Begin with the cube roots one, omega, and omega squared. Their exact relation is one plus omega plus omega squared equals zero.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Suppose omega had residue one. Its square would too. If instead omega squared had residue one, squaring it gives omega, again of residue one. In either case, reducing the exact relation would give one plus one plus one equals zero. In characteristic two, a pair of ones disappears and the third remains. That would say one equals zero, impossible in a nonzero ring. The two nontrivial candidates are excluded.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Now take the general case. Let A be a characteristic-zero domain, with a ring map into a nonzero ring of characteristic two. Let alpha have odd order r and residue one. Order one already means alpha equals one. Suppose instead that alpha is not one, so r is greater than one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Work in the original domain. Factor the difference between alpha to the r and one as alpha minus one times the sum of the first r powers, starting at one. The power alpha to the r is one, so this product is zero. Since alpha minus one is nonzero and A is a domain, the geometric sum itself is zero. This cancellation takes place before reduction.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Only now apply the ring map. Every power of alpha becomes one. Write the odd integer r as two k plus one. The sum has k pairs of ones, each equal to zero in characteristic two, and one term left over. The exact vanishing sum would therefore give zero equals one. This contradicts the target ring being nonzero. Alpha must equal one. The target ring need not be a domain; we cancelled only in the original domain.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
<p>Apply the lemma separately to R, Delta of d divided by Delta of c, and to W, Delta of g divided by Delta of c. They belong to A, since their evaluated factors are units there. Node six point one gives odd order. Node six point three gives residue one in the nonzero quotient established in Node six point two. Combining those two inputs yields the exact identities R equals one and W equals one. These are arithmetic conclusions, with no estimate of complex distance. We can now unpack the comparison products while keeping the original evaluation points distinct.<break time="1200ms"/></p>
</speak>
